 heating causes casein to coagulate and the Mohi starts forming clumps. The Datshi is collected in a cloth and squeezed to eliminate extra whey and then rolled into appropriate sized cheese balls."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-datshi"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Datshi"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Bhutanese cuisine","cuisine/bhutanese-cuisine",[["Ema datshi","dish/ema-datshi"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/82/Datshi.jpg/500px-Datshi.jpg","w":500,"h":568,"by":"Nair A, Choden D, Pradhan M.","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Datshi.jpg"}},"dish/finnan-haddie":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/finnan-haddie","n":"Finnan haddie","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Scottish cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["A Finnan haddie is a haddock that has been cured with the smoke of green wood or peat. They are usually said to have originated in Findon, a fishing village south of Aberdeen, though an alternative tradition traces them to Findhorn in Moray."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-finnan-haddie"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finnan_haddie"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Scottish cuisine","cuisine/scottish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Haddock","ingredient/haddock"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Scottish Scran","ref":"site/scottishscran-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"scottishscran.com","note":"Blog working through traditional Scottish dishes one recipe at a time.","url":"https://scottishscran.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Something Sweet Something Savoury: Scottish recipes","ref":"site/somethingsweetsomethingsavoury-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub